1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:03,125
Strike a bell, and it rings at its own notes.

2
00:00:03,125 --> 00:00:10,100
Strike a crystal with a very short laser pulse, and its atoms ring too, swaying together at their own frequencies.

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:12,625
But they do it trillions of times a second.

4
00:00:12,625 --> 00:00:14,675
No camera is that fast.

5
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:17,813
A beamline uses two pulses.

6
00:00:17,813 --> 00:00:20,762
The laser pulse, the pump, starts the ringing.

7
00:00:20,762 --> 00:00:29,400
An X-ray pulse, the probe, arrives a set delay later, and the X-rays it scatters onto a detector record where the atoms are at that instant.

8
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:35,300
Then it repeats, with the delay a little longer each time: one detector frame per delay.

9
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:39,737
Frames taken without the laser show the crystal at rest.

10
00:00:39,737 --> 00:00:46,462
The beam's own brightness wobbles from pulse to pulse, so each frame is divided by a monitor of it first.

11
00:00:46,462 --> 00:00:55,150
Then compare laser on with laser off, delay by delay, and what's left is what the laser changed: a trace that rings, and dies away.

12
00:00:56,050 --> 00:01:00,638
xRay fits the trace as a sum of damped oscillations, called modes.

13
00:01:00,638 --> 00:01:05,200
Each mode has a frequency, a rate at which it dies away, and a strength.

14
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:08,450
Here there are two, and together they follow the trace.

15
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:15,275
Each row of the detector sees the crystal from a slightly different angle, and rings at its own frequency.

16
00:01:15,275 --> 00:01:18,413
On a GPU, xRay fits every row.

17
00:01:18,413 --> 00:01:25,112
In this module, you'll fit one trace whose answer you know, find out how a mode can go missing, and fit a whole detector.

18
00:01:25,112 --> 00:01:27,275
A fit always returns modes.

19
00:01:27,275 --> 00:01:29,575
The question is whether they're all there.
